\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{Notation for Gaussian laws}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The bar #{\overline\lambda} is conjugation and the declared
involution (\ref{fdip-0024}, \ref{fdip-00QP}).}

\p{The measure in an integral is #{\extd}
(\ref{fdip-002K}, \ref{fdip-00QR}).}

\p{An expectation is #{\langle F\rangle_Q}
(\ref{fdip-0006}, \ref{fdip-00QU}).}

\p{A complex unit supplied by the algebra is #{\ui}
(\ref{fdip-0027}, \ref{fdip-00QW}).}

\p{Euler's number is #{\ue} (\ref{fdip-002S}, \ref{fdip-00QX}).}

\p{A probability law pairs with an observable as a cochain pairs
with a chain, so an expectation is written with the angle brackets
of that pairing, #{\langle F\rangle_Q}. The measure in an integral
shares #{\extd} with the derivative; the integral sign tells them
apart. Euler's number #{\ue} and a complex unit #{\ui} are kept
distinct from an edge or basis element #{e} and an index #{i}. A
complex scalar appears only where the problem supplies it.}
